WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.773
Welcome to Module Eight, Lesson Two: drift, regression, and the maintenance loop.

2
00:00:05.103 --> 00:00:07.719
Agents degrade even when nobody touches them.

3
00:00:08.519 --> 00:00:13.312
Picture CampusDesk, a university helpdesk agent that answers student questions.

4
00:00:13.642 --> 00:00:16.216
All summer, nobody changes a line of its code.

5
00:00:17.016 --> 00:00:19.739
By September, its answers are noticeably worse.

6
00:00:20.069 --> 00:00:22.366
The world around it changed, and that's drift.

7
00:00:23.166 --> 00:00:26.060
Input drift: students ask new things, in new ways.

8
00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:29.753
Spot it by tracking query topics and embeddings over time.

9
00:00:30.553 --> 00:00:34.642
Corpus drift: the wiki was reorganised, so retrieval quality moved.

10
00:00:34.972 --> 00:00:38.911
Re-run retrieval evaluation on a fixed set of queries to catch it.

11
00:00:39.711 --> 00:00:43.459
Model drift: the provider silently updated its hosted model.

12
00:00:43.789 --> 00:00:48.069
Pin versions to prevent it, and run a golden set continuously to detect it.

13
00:00:48.869 --> 00:00:52.318
Tool drift: the enrollment system's API changed its schema.

14
00:00:52.648 --> 00:00:55.819
Scheduled contract tests against every tool catch that.

15
00:00:56.619 --> 00:01:00.708
So run the golden set against production on a schedule, not only in CI.

16
00:01:01.038 --> 00:01:03.399
That catches drift that no code change caused.

17
00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:06.807
Sample live traffic for automated scoring,

18
00:01:06.857 --> 00:01:09.845
and send uncertain cases to a small human queue.

19
00:01:10.175 --> 00:01:12.258
Track every metric against a baseline.

20
00:01:13.058 --> 00:01:17.531
When an incident hits, first contain it with a previous version or a safe fallback.

21
00:01:17.861 --> 00:01:20.627
A degraded answer beats a confidently wrong one.

22
00:01:21.427 --> 00:01:26.155
Next, diagnose from traces: pull the failing runs and find the common span.

23
00:01:26.485 --> 00:01:31.193
Then fix the right layer, whether prompt, schema, tool, retrieval, or model version.

24
00:01:31.993 --> 00:01:35.057
Add the failing case to the golden set, or you'll ship it again.

25
00:01:35.387 --> 00:01:37.300
Then ask what guard would have caught it sooner.

26
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.807
Version prompts, tool schemas, retrieval settings, models, and guardrails together.

27
00:01:43.137 --> 00:01:46.970
Record that version on every trace, so any output can be reproduced.

28
00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:49.544
Gate changes on the golden set,

29
00:01:49.594 --> 00:01:53.309
then roll out through shadow and canary, with a tested rollback.

30
00:01:53.639 --> 00:01:56.597
Even a one-word prompt edit is a production change.

31
00:01:57.397 --> 00:01:58.071
On the exam,

32
00:01:58.121 --> 00:02:02.595
an agent that degrades with no deployment means something upstream changed.

33
00:02:02.925 --> 00:02:05.286
The fix is pinned versions, a golden set,

34
00:02:05.336 --> 00:02:08.166
and contract tests, so read on for the full loop.
